<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">March of Verona</h1><p class="st-meta">952 CE – 1164 CE (approx.) · capital Verona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Imperial march in north-eastern Italy created by Otto I in 952, which joined Verona, Friuli and Istria to the German duchies until the Veronese cities rose against Barbarossa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 952 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-ii-of-italy">Berengar II</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, to cede <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-verona">Verona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Istria</a>. Otto attached these lands to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria</a> under his brother Henry, creating a march that secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner</a> and eastern Alpine routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Italy. In 976 it passed with the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-carinthia">Duchy of Carinthia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The march was held by German dukes and margraves who rarely resided there, among them Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berthold-i-of-zahringen">Berthold of Zähringen</a>; Hermann I, margrave of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-verona">Verona</a> in the 1060s, passed the title to his heirs, the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. Real power in the region shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, the patriarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> and the growing city of Verona.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1164 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-verona">Verona</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-vicenza">Vicenza</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-padua">Padua</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-treviso">Treviso</a> formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veronese-league">Veronese League</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, and the league later merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lombard-league">Lombard League</a>. The march ceased to function, and Verona became a self-governing commune. The margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> kept the title.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/march-of-verona.jpg" alt="March of Verona" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: New Marco · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_March_of_Verona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>952 CE – 1164 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Verona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry I of Bavaria, Hermann I of Baden</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Old High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>March of Verona and Aquileia, Margraviate of Verona</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1058&amp;focus=march-of-verona" aria-label="March of Verona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4941 575 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where March of Verona lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5261 831L5308 809M5261 831L5279 723M5261 831L5336 795M5261 831L5229 694M5261 831L5261 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5308 809h0M5279 723h0M5336 79